    98	   agents must understand their instructions)
    99	10. The calibration protocol — rerun against the archived spec, score
   100	    against S1–S9 as ground truth
   101	
   102	Source evidence: the one-off subtraction review `claude-subtract-fable.md`
   103	(Fable, off-grid, user-requested 2026-08-10) and the user's nine rulings on
   104	it, recorded under Group S in `dispositions.md`. Status: the subtract cell
   105	is NOT part of the md-review skill or grid — the user ruled it joins only
   106	after prompt improvement and a calibration test, and that addition is an
   107	instruction-class change walked with him. This file is the input to that
   108	future prompt draft.
   109	
   110	Scorecard: 6 of 9 cuts accepted (S3, S4, S5, S7, S8, S9), 3 rejected
   111	(S1, S2, S6). Every rejection shared one shape: the reviewer traded
   112	operational robustness for present textual/code smallness. Every
   113	acceptance removed something with no consumer or a duplicated carrier.
   114	Small sample — nine findings, one document — which is itself the argument
   115	for the calibration protocol below.
   116	
   117	## What the prompt lacked (each traced to a correction)
   118	
   119	1. **The optimization axis was never stated** — the root cause, in the
   120	   user's own words at S6: "asking to simplify is like asking to optimize
   121	   without context — faster, cheaper, more reliable, simpler to write,
   122	   simpler to maintain, simpler to use?" The prompt must state the
   123	   project's axis explicitly: simple-to-operate beats simple-to-build;
   124	   mechanical guarantees beat trained habit; build cost is acceptable when
   125	   it buys maintenance-free operation. Require every proposed cut to name
   126	   which axis it optimizes and which it sacrifices — the trade visible on
   127	   its face.
   128	2. **No roadmap context** (S1's rejection). The reviewer quoted "there are
   129	   no slow checks yet" as grounds to cut the async machinery, never asking
   130	   whether slow checks were *expected*. They were — tests and reviews are
   131	   planned. Rule for the prompt: provide the forward plan, and a mechanism
   132	   whose deferral trigger is expected to fire is not a valid cut; "build
   133	   the machinery while the system is still simple" is the standing
   134	   preference.
   135	3. **"No consumer" misapplied to forcing functions** (S6's rejection). The
   136	   reviewer argued nothing reads the `Gatekeeper-issue` trailer — true and
   137	   beside the point: the required field's value is the *forced explicit
   138	   answer* (issue number or deliberate `none`), a mechanical guarantee
   139	   that would otherwise degrade to LLM habit. Rule for the prompt: "who is
   140	   forced to decide something because this exists?" counts as a consumer;
   141	   never propose replacing a deterministic mechanism with trained agent
   142	   behavior.
   143	4. **Operator cost vs builder cost conflated** (S2's rejection). Cutting
   144	   the self-updating deployed copy would have reintroduced a remembered
   145	   human step (manual `sudo cp`) — a regression on the stated axis dressed
   146	   as a simplification. Rule: a cut that adds a recurring human obligation
   147	   is not a subtraction; it moves cost from build-time to forever.
   148	
   149	## What worked — keep these instructions verbatim in spirit
   150	
   151	5. **The validated cut classes** (all six accepted cuts fit one):
   152	   detectors/outputs with no consumer (S3, S5); duplicated normative homes
   153	   (S4); carrier-vs-invariant collapse — derive the fact in one place,
   154	   never drop the fact (S7); guards that guard nothing (S8); dead code and
   155	   distinction-carrying names with no machine consumer (S9). Enumerate
   156	   these as the classes to hunt.
   157	6. **Refute-your-own-candidates before reporting.** The refuted list
   158	   (digest dedup, --base outright, --agent, the advisory, slice-3
   159	   concurrency) was as valuable as the findings — it certified the lean
   160	   core and saved walk time.
   161	7. **Flag collisions with recorded rulings instead of dodging them.** The
   162	   reviewer's "two user rulings get contradicted — surfacing that is the
   163	   point of this review" framing was correct and the user engaged with it.
   164	
   165	## New requirements (not discussed in the walk, learned from its aftermath)
   166	
   167	8. **Consequence sweep per cut**: enumerate every spec sentence that
   168	   becomes false or stale when the cut lands. S3's application left "the
   169	   raw-push residual is detected at its source" standing — caught only by
   170	   the later Codex leg (its FIX-3). The reviewer proposing a cut holds the
   171	   full blast-radius in view once and should deliver it with the finding.
   172	9. **Grounds must be quoted, not paraphrased**: each WHY that leans on the
   173	   document's own text quotes it, so triage can verify without re-deriving
   174	   (the one-off mostly did this; make it mandatory).
   175	
   176	## Calibration protocol before any grid seat
   177	
   178	Run the improved prompt against the archived pre-walk spec revision
   179	(commit 0890848's `git-gatekeeper-design.md`) and score against the user's
   180	S1–S9 rulings as ground truth: the six accepted cuts should surface; the
   181	three rejected cuts should either not surface or surface with their
   182	axis-cost stated honestly (a finding that says "this trades operational
   183	reliability for less code — likely against the project's axis" is a pass,
   184	not a miss). Repeat on a second document before proposing the grid
   185	addition, which is walked with the user like any skill change.

