./md-review-records/2026-08-04-d-review-skill-self-review/claude-restate-good.md:281:2. "Claude-runtime cells are fresh subagents — good = the pinned top model at high effort, floor = the pinned floor model (Sonnet-class today), set per launch." — This specifies how cells are realized on the Claude runtime: each is a newly spawned subagent. The "good" tier is defined as the model pinned as the top model, run with a high reasoning-effort setting; the "floor" tier is defined as whichever model is pinned as the floor model, which the parenthetical says is currently a model in the Sonnet class. "Set per launch" is ambiguous to me between two readings: (a) these pinned values are established at each launch of the review, so they can differ from run to run; or (b) the tier settings are applied when each cell is launched. I lean toward (a) given the surrounding emphasis on pinning, but the sentence does not settle it.
./md-review-records/2026-08-04-d-review-skill-self-review/claude-restate-good.md:283:3. "Codex-runtime cells run through [`scripts/d-review-codex-cell.py`](../../../scripts/d-review-codex-cell.py) — one invocation per cell (`--cell restate|defect-hunt --tier good|floor --target <path>`), headless `codex exec`, read-only sandbox, the cell's final message on stdout." — This specifies how cells are realized on the Codex runtime: they are executed by running the named Python script, at the repository path `scripts/d-review-codex-cell.py`. Four operational facts follow: the script is invoked once per cell, not once for a batch; the invocation takes three arguments, `--cell` set to either `restate` or `defect-hunt`, `--tier` set to either `good` or `floor`, and `--target` set to the document's path; the underlying execution is a headless (non-interactive) `codex exec` run; the sandbox the cell runs in is read-only, so the cell cannot modify anything; and the cell's result is delivered as its final message printed to standard output.
./md-review-records/2026-08-04-d-review-skill-self-review/claude-restate-good.md:287:5. "The tier-to-model mapping and per-tier reasoning effort sit at the top of the script (authoritative; any ids quoted in prose are a snapshot), currently `gpt-5.6-sol` / `gpt-5.6-terra` at `xhigh`, boss-picked and live-verified 2026-08-03, pinned in the script so a cell does not depend on the machine-local Codex config." — This locates the authoritative definition of which model serves which tier, and the reasoning-effort level for each tier, at the top of the `d-review-codex-cell.py` script. The parenthetical establishes precedence: the script is authoritative, and any model identifiers written in prose (including in this sentence) are only a point-in-time snapshot that may be out of date. The current values are given as `gpt-5.6-sol` and `gpt-5.6-terra`, both at reasoning effort `xhigh`; the sentence does not say which of the two is "good" and which is "floor." These were chosen by the boss and verified against a live run on 2026-08-03. The final clause gives the reason for hard-coding them in the script: so that a cell's behavior does not vary with whatever Codex configuration happens to exist on the local machine.
