./md-review-records/2026-08-12-attack-split-experiment/fast-handoff-fresh-eyes-claude.md:11:- **Reaper (script, run by cron/systemd timer every minute; flock per session).** Not a daemon; a scan. For each registered session: if `handoff.requested` and `note.done` exist and the session is idle (last transcript record is a completed turn, no recent user input), kill and relaunch. If the note hasn't appeared after a grace period (session confused or hung), kill anyway — the fallback path covers it. The reaper is also the fleet watchdog for free: a session whose transcript hasn't moved in N minutes, or whose PID is gone, gets the same treatment. Interactive sessions are recycled in place with `tmux respawn-pane -k` so the boss's layout survives; headless ones are re-exec'd from registry flags.
./md-review-records/2026-08-12-attack-split-experiment/fast-handoff-fresh-eyes-claude.md:43:- **The wedged-but-light session.** Token fill isn't the only degradation: sessions get confused, loop, or stall well under threshold, and the boss ends up hand-recycling those — the "no human typing" goal quietly fails. A stall/error-rate trigger into the *same* recycle path is cheap at design time.
