./docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:8:- **Order:** when a document gets both reviews, md-review runs first, the sanity-check second — the checker reads the post-review text so its findings (and the Fable tokens behind them) go to the design, not to prose problems md-review fixes more cheaply. "md-review passed" is the natural moment to ask whether the document deserves its deliberate sanity-check.
./docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:11:- **Models (user-set): Fable and gpt-5.6-sol at xhigh, in parallel, on every attack.** Both runtimes run each attack cell; per-attack, per-runtime scoring against the ground-truth documents, with unions and unique finds recorded. The Codex leg draws its own budget rather than the Fable pool, so the second head is quota-relief, not quota-cost. Any later downgrade (a cheaper model on a narrower attack) is earned by the same scoring method before adoption.
./docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:12:- **The validation experiment gates the seat:** three attacks × two runtimes × the two archived ground-truth documents (`reviewed-spec-at-0890848.md`, `reviewed-fast-handoff-design.md`), the fresh-eyes attack receiving problem statements only. Scored against the recorded rulings (S1–S9 and the seven fast-handoff rulings): union coverage vs the unsplit head (the cross-lens F1 is the marked case), unique finds per cell, false-finding rate. Scorecards land beside the calibration records; the seat goes to the shape the record favors — the unsplit prompt keeping it is a legitimate outcome.
./md-review-records/2026-08-09-ghi-info-agent-design-2/claude-restate-floor.md:72:9. This overall approach is not merely theoretical — it is already proven, because a separate project called nedsmessenger runs this exact same pattern live in production: specifically, a function called `ask_claude` in a file at `~/Projects/nedsmessenger/adapter/adapter.py` invokes Claude headlessly using `claude -p --resume` for each conversation, extracts the answer from the process's exit output, and includes watchdog mechanisms that detect and handle runs that get stuck.
./md-review-records/2026-08-09-ghi-info-agent-design-2/claude-restate-floor.md:125:5. Building a system of multiple watchdog processes for supervision is deliberately excluded, because a single overall timeout is sufficient at the current scale of one question at a time; this would only be reconsidered if the single timeout proves too imprecise in practice.
./docs/drafts/md-review-tooling-design.md:7:1. **`scripts/md-review-claude-cell.py`** — Claude-runtime cell launcher, twin of the Codex one.
./docs/drafts/ghi-info-agent-plan-draft.md:54:- Three watchdogs kill a stuck run — idle silence, a silent in-flight tool call, and a total-runtime backstop — so a hung agent cannot block its caller forever.
