     9	Slices 1 to 5 of five are built (slice 5's CLAUDE.md workflow lines land
    10	separately, walked with the user). For each request the program does exactly
    11	one of two things — checks the work in, or refuses and teaches the fix. On
    12	success four things are true: the change is on main, the checks ran against
    13	exactly the content pushed, the commit's trailers carry the whole
    14	machine-readable record, and the caller has the commit id.
    15	
    16	Built: a synchronous check-in end to end (slice 1); the entry checkpoint — the
    17	recorded gate every legacy import crosses (slice 2; the import record is read
    18	straight from history with `git log origin/main --grep "Gatekeeper-import:"` —
    19	an `imports` table subcommand was built here and deleted by user ruling
    20	2026-08-10, the trailer being the record and the git command the view); and
    21	concurrent check-ins, where a request that loses the race is integrated over
    22	the newer commits by the program rather than by the calling agent (slice 3).
    23	
    24	Check-ins run in parallel with no queue and no lock, because GitHub already
    25	provides the one property the design rests on: a push either wins cleanly or
    26	is rejected whole. The winner never learns there was a race. The loser fetches
    27	the new main and re-applies its declared changes onto it, which is clean
    28	whenever the two requests touched different paths — the usual case. When they
    29	touched the same path, re-applying would mean choosing whose version survives,
    30	so the request is refused as `conflict` instead: the program never guesses at
    31	an author's intent.
    32	
    33	Slice 4 (built 2026-08-12): --no-wait detaches a worker into its own
    34	session (process group), whose outcome lands in history on success or as
    35	the retained B4d refusal record on refusal; status answers checked-in /
    36	in-progress / abandoned / the retained record (once, then swept) / unknown;
    37	cancel kills the worker's whole process group, waits, then lets history
    38	arbitrate — four outcomes. Every invocation opportunistically sweeps stale
    39	workspaces (30-day refusal records, day-old screening scratch and
    40	dead-worker leftovers). Slice 5 (built 2026-08-12): the branch-protection
    41	audit — `audit` reads main's live protection via gh and answers B3c's three
    42	outcomes, protection-ok / protection-wrong (facts naming every differing
    43	setting) / audit-failed (gh missing, unauthenticated, API error — a loud
    44	finding, never a silent skip); it rides each session recycle via the
    45	fast-handoff writer. Exit code 2 stays reserved for a defect in this
    46	program; the parser layer refuses malformed command lines as JSON, exit 1.
    47	
    48	The entry checkpoint's guarantee is that the record cannot lag the system: an
    49	import is declared as a triple, validated against the legacy repository at
    50	instant screening, and written into the trailer of the very commit that
    51	carries it. A second import in one request is inexpressible by construction —
    52	it is a second check-in.
    53	
    54	Resubmitting is always safe. The digest identifies the WORK — base, paths and
    55	their bytes — and not the metadata around it, so identical work resubmitted
    56	under a different message deduplicates, and work that already went through
    57	answers already-checked-in with its commit id. An agent that crashed never
    58	reconstructs what happened; it submits again.
    59	
    60	The base — the main commit the work started from — is computed by the
    61	program (`git merge-base HEAD origin/main`, after a fetch, in the caller's
    62	checkout), never declared: user ruling 2026-08-10, replacing a --base field
    63	and its two hand-off refusals. Same exact id, no relay step to garble.
    64	
    65	Reply contract (B1): exactly one JSON object on stdout, always carrying a
    66	human-readable `summary`. Exit 0 for success and informational answers, 1 for
    67	a catalog refusal (the gatekeeper working correctly), 2 for a program defect.
    68	
    69	Records: git history and the invoking session's transcript, and nothing else.
    70	The workspace exists only while a request is in flight and is swept on both
    71	endings, so a refusal leaves the repository and the disk untouched.
    72	
    73	Usage:
    74	  git-gatekeeper.py check-in --files <path>... --message <text>
    75	                    --issue none|<n> --agent <runtime/model>
    76	                    (--import none | --import-commit <40-hex>
