docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:10:This revision (2026-08-09, amended through 2026-08-12) folds in: the two pending amendments from the 2026-07-30 bindings walk (the refused `--no-wait` workspace retains a JSON refusal record, `status` returns it once then sweeps; the branch-protection audit's three named outcomes); the `Gatekeeper-agent` trailer (B6, boss-ruled 2026-07-31); and the credential rulings of 2026-08-09 (C1–C8 — the dedicated identity admitted early, the Unix-user boundary, scoped agent tokens, break-glass, the cooperative hook tier; their delta document, `docs/issues/queue/3-gatekeeper-credential-and-hook-bindings.md`, was retired 2026-08-10 when this specification became the rulings' sole normative home — provenance in git history and `md-review-records/`). The 2026-07-24 boss-walked core — request/reply, digest, trailers, concurrency, states, error catalog — is unchanged by all of this. Supersedes remain as before: the promotion-relay design (an earlier two-stage shape where changes reached main through a relay branch — replaced by this single gate), the entry-manifest append-a-row rule, the retired "land"/"landing" vocabulary (check-in vocabulary replaces it). The B-codes cited throughout (B1, B3c, B4a–B4d, B6) are the 2026-07-30 bindings-walk rulings, recorded in `docs/issues/queue/3-gatekeeper-build-bindings.md`. Throughout this document, "the boss" and "the user" are the same person: the human owner of the project.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-11-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-12-How a change reaches main in nedschorus. **Scope ends at main** (boss-ruled 2026-07-24): deploying main to production is a separate DevOps/CI concern, designed when the first long-running process exists — until then main is production coincidentally, not by this design's doing. Ordinary changes use no branches and no pull requests.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-13-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-14-## The job and the guarantee
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-15-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:16:One program, `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py`, is the only way any change reaches main. It holds the project's one **main-capable** credential (this section states the contract; the credential is dormant until it exists — see Implementation status). Agents — all of them, equally — invoke the program directly and never push to main themselves (branch pushes stay open to agent tokens — C4). One program, one credential, one door. No agent has a doorman or relay role in another agent's check-in path; choirmaster is simply the most frequent requester.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-17-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:18:For each check-in request the program does exactly one of two things: **checks the work in**, or **refuses and teaches the fix**. (Informational subcommands — `status`, `cancel` — answer rather than check in; § The reply.)
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-19-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-20-On success, four things are guaranteed true:
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-21-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-22-1. The change is on main, pushed to GitHub.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:23:2. The checks ran against exactly the content that was pushed.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-24-3. The commit's trailer lines carry the whole machine-readable record (see § The trailer).
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-25-4. The requester has the answer: success plus the commit id (directly in the waiting form; a non-waiting caller collects the same answer via `status`).
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-26-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:27:On refusal, the reply always has three parts, like a well-built MCP tool — never a bare error code: the **named error** from the fixed catalog, the **specific facts** (which file, which check, which mismatch), and the **exact next action, written for an agent to execute**. The caller's files and main are untouched — a refusal changes no worktree content and lands nothing. Scoped exactly (2026-08-12): the base computation's fetch updates the caller's remote-tracking refs (metadata, never files); any invocation may sweep aged refusal records (§ States); a refused `--no-wait` request retains a JSON refusal record until read or expired (B4d); and a push whose acknowledgment was lost can leave the commit on main behind a `network-down` reply — resubmitting self-heals to `already-checked-in`.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-28-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-29-**Records:** git history and the invoking session's ordinary transcript are the *only* durable records. No side files, no separate logs — the invocation is a normal tool call, already recorded where tool calls are recorded. (Transient, not a record: the B4d refusal record above, which exists only until collected or swept.)
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-30-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:31:**Resubmitting is always safe.** Same request, same answer (infrastructure weather aside: a `network-down` retry may answer `already-checked-in` where the first attempt reported failure — the self-healing working, not a contradiction); a fixed request digests differently and processes fresh; work that already went through answers `already-checked-in <commit>`. An agent that crashed or lost its connection never reconstructs what happened — it submits again and the program sorts it out. Refusals teach, retries are free: the loop self-heals, which is what near-perfect autonomous operation requires.
