docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:170:RESOLVED 2026-07-24, then SUPERSEDED 2026-08-02 by the session-recycling revision of [fast-handoff-design.md](fast-handoff-design.md): recycling handoffs are machine-local and never checked in; the one committed handoff (the founding one) lands as an ordinary file. The files-written-to-disk-first principle survives inside the supervisor's cycle.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-171-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-172-## Relationship to the legacy design
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-173-
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md-174-The legacy system's `git-clean-slate-plan.md` (read-only reference, `~/Projects/nedlern/docs/working/proposed/`) designed the many-writer version of this problem. This section is the one classification record for the rewrite policy (founding plan § Standing decisions), in its four-class vocabulary. This design keeps only two of its ideas, re-derived (**update-feature**): workflow rules expressed as CLAUDE.md documentation, and protection-as-lock reduced to one credential. Never imported (**remove-feature** — multi-writer machinery; this project has one writer): the three GitHub Apps, the credential helper, per-agent branches, the PR pipeline for ordinary work, the parking states. The repo's git config is minimal and stated here, not imported (**preserve-feature**, contract kept and values re-derived): `user.name`/`user.email` for the machine identity, `useConfigOnly` so no global identity leaks in. No consider-feature entries, so nothing goes to `legacy-feature-queue/`.
