docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-14-## The components
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-15-
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-16-Each script's module docstring says what it does and why it is shaped that way; each has a `…-test.py` suite beside it stating what must keep passing.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-17-
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md:18:- `scripts/handoff-context-threshold-hook.py` — the auto-trigger: a Stop hook that reads context used from the session's own transcript and tells the agent to run the skill at the threshold.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-19-- `.claude/skills/handoff/SKILL.md` — the skill: the agent writes `next-step`, runs the writer, does what it reports. Walked and landed 2026-08-06 (dispositions in `docs/drafts/handoff-skill-draft.md`).
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-20-- `scripts/handoff-write-and-check-supervisor.py` — the writer: fills every field a machine can compute, writes the handoff file, reports supervisor liveness.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-21-- `scripts/handoff-supervisor.py` — the supervisor: watch, kill, extract, pre-seed tasks, launch the successor with the ignition prompt; one per agent, self-registered, lock-guarded.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-22-- `scripts/handoff-extract-conversation.py` — the extractor: carries the word-floor tail of the dialog verbatim to the successor.
--
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-32-- **The agent supplies only `next-step`; the writer fills every field a machine can compute (user-ruled 2026-08-06).** Hand-written fields asked the agent to do arithmetic and left silent-truncation failures open; the writer's mechanisms (file-not-argument, whitespace collapse, counter from the max of file and consumed values, refuse-empty) each answer a named observed failure, recorded in its docstring.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-33-- **The retiring agent exercises no judgment over what its successor receives (user-ruled 2026-08-06).** The extractor's word-floor tail is the recycling boundary — the floor value and its sizing rationale live in the extractor — extended back to the nearest user prompt; `--boundary-quote` survives as a manual override only. The 5-topics rule, the "err long" hedge, and the boundary field died with this ruling.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-34-- **`next-step` is an instruction to act on, not a summary, and every pointer carries a pin** (path + commit SHA, repository + issue number) so the successor resolves what the writer meant. Text of record: the skill (2026-08-06).
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-35-- **The skill runs one script that does everything best done by script (user-ruled 2026-08-06).** The writer and the liveness report are one command because they are one decision: a handoff nobody is watching must not stop the agent working, and an agent that runs only half of a two-step procedure would stop anyway.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md:36:- **Self-registration, not discovery (user-asked 2026-08-06).** The agent starts its own supervisor when its handoff script finds none watching; nothing scans the machine. Two questions dissolve structurally: only sessions carrying the skill ever get a supervisor, and subagents raise `SubagentStop`, not `Stop`, so the hook never fires for them. A per-agent lock refuses a second supervisor (two would double-kill and double-launch). Adoption also closed the bootstrap hole: a hand-started session can be picked up and recycled.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-37-- **The founding boot is one committed file, no standing machinery.** Launched with the ruled prompt pattern (`claude "$(cat <path>)"` — a launcher passes the prompt; CLAUDE.md instructions do not wake a session, [nedschorus#27](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/27)). A boss-called durable snapshot is an ordinary commit on request.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md:38:- **The auto-trigger reads the transcript, and only the transcript (user-ruled 2026-08-12).** The statusline relay — a second data source riding the interactive-only statusline — was cut: its sole remaining trigger was a session whose first turn had not completed, a moment the threshold cannot be crossed. The statusline renderer survives as `scripts/session-statusline-command.py`, outside this system.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md:39:- **The hook fires at the threshold unconditionally (user-ruled 2026-08-12).** Its supervisor-liveness gate was cut: self-registration made an unwatched firing self-healing (the writer starts an adopting supervisor), so silence could only turn a dead supervisor into a permanently un-recycled session. Per-agent wiring of the hook, if ever wanted, waits on an agent-naming convention and gates on being a named agent, not on liveness.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-40-- **The queue-status line rides the ignition prompt (user-ruled 2026-08-12).** The #32 rot-visibility duty (queue depth and oldest item, visible to the boss) discharged into a log file under detached and headless supervisors; every successor now receives the line with the instruction to surface anything rotting. The console print stays for a watched pane.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-41-- **No scheduled canary re-runs (user-ruled 2026-08-12).** Task pre-seed rides undocumented harness state, and the user does not care about the risk of a Claude Code upgrade breaking it. Detection is the successor's ignition count-check (trial-proven to fire unprompted) with the queues as backstop; the `--canary` cases in the supervisor suite are the diagnosis when it fires.
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-42-
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-43-## Verified facts (2026-07-21 – 2026-08-06, Claude Code v2.1.220)
--
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-48-| `--allowedTools` on the launch silently prevents the positional prompt from firing — never combine them in a launcher | canary (2×2) |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-49-| An agent cannot `/clear` or `/exit` itself; self-SIGTERM trips the safety classifier (exit 143) | canary |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-50-| Every assistant transcript record carries `message.usage` (input + cache-read + cache-creation tokens = that request's prompt size) and `message.model`; context used is therefore computable from the transcript alone, in any session | probe 2026-08-06 |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-51-| A headless (`claude -p`) session NEVER runs the status line; statusline stdin carries `.context_window.remaining_percentage`, Stop-hook stdin does not (why the trigger reads the transcript) | probe |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md:52:| The model→context-window table lives in the threshold hook and must be re-checked when a new model ships; an unknown model id takes the 200K default, which over-fires on larger windows — the safe direction | hook source |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-53-| Tasks are `<N>.json` under `~/.claude/tasks/<session-id>/`; the dir is created lazily; `--session-id <uuid>` forces the id; pre-seed canaries 1 and 2 passed | probe/canary |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-54-| A task record's `id` is a STRING, and `blocks`/`blockedBy` arrays are present; a record with an integer id is dropped by TaskList while still counting toward the next allocated id — so a schema-wrong pre-seed looks half-successful | canary 2026-08-06 |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-55-| `--continue` keeps the prior session id; a context clear mints a new id; the session id is in the environment | measured |
docs/cross-project/fast-handoff-design.md-56-| A fresh subagent's context floor is CLAUDE.md + prompt | measured |
