14:{"parentUuid":"afededa1-3461-4d98-a6b7-f91251998c42","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdwZG8r4TaJK6JHSm97hW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018B2fRmuk6Vq3V5GDAQx2EE","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/subtract-cell-prompt-lessons.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9552,"cache_read_input_tokens":23038,"output_tokens":141,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9552,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":141,"cache_read_input_tokens":23038,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9552,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9552},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdwZG4dJwL6a4RXdJLGrE","type":"assistant","uuid":"6ca719aa-ba67-46a1-b118-f04aedcd4779","timestamp":"2026-08-11T20:01:41.857Z","effort":"high","session_id":"d9eda3ec-c327-4154-a2c1-ab5796f1ce65","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster","sessionId":"29d66917-9767-47cb-a221-d4876d8014cd","version":"2.1.227","gitBranch":"choirmaster"}
15:{"parentUuid":"6ca719aa-ba67-46a1-b118-f04aedcd4779","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d0d8f073-7035-45e9-97cf-cdbcda5ec8a8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018B2fRmuk6Vq3V5GDAQx2EE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Subtraction-reviewer prompt lessons — from the S1–S9 walk corrections\n2\t\n3\t## Walk order\n4\t\n5\tWALK CLOSED 2026-08-11 at item 2 (user direction): rather than continue\n6\titem-by-item, a fresh context redrafts the reviewer instructions and a\n7\tNEW walk starts from scratch over that draft. The user's assessment of\n8\tthe first merged draft: it has good content but keys the whole document\n9\toff the wrong word — \"simple\" does not appear to be the right concept to\n10\torganize around; his ruled candidate name is the **sanity-checker** (refined from\n11\t\"sanity reviewer\" the same day).\n12\tThe redraft reads everything, including the rejected draft (knowing its\n13\tkeyword framing is wrong), and this file's body remains the requirements\n14\trecord. Items 3–10 below were never walked; their content feeds the new\n15\tdraft and the new walk.\n16\t\n17\tRe-planned 2026-08-11 mid-walk: item 2's discussion produced a complete\n18\tmerged prompt draft (`docs/drafts/simplification-review-prompt-draft.md`\n19\t— the user's axis statement + choirmaster's additions + Codex's\n20\tnaming/vocabulary notes, per the user's combine-all-three direction), so\n21\titems 3–8 become confirmations of that draft's sections, and the user\n22\tadded an md-review of the settled draft before the calibration run.\n23\t\n24\t1. Purpose: what these rulings produce and the bar they meet\n25\t   processed 2026-08-11 → accepted; capture is the walk-order block.\n26\t2. The core prompt: axis, concept, method\n27\t   open 2026-08-11 — revised twice (TRADE line withdrawn as unhelpful —\n28\t   the flawed run's LOST field already declared costs honestly, the\n29\t   failure was weighing, which the axis statement fixes; then the user\n30\t   directed a full three-source merge, complete not condensed, keeping\n31\t   simplicity as the central concept with reliability/testability as\n32\t   what it must deliver). First merged draft REJECTED 2026-08-11: it\n33\t   tried to overwrite the meaning of \"simple\" (opening by redefining the\n34\t   word away from its everyday sense), which the user ruled against; he\n35\t   also judged the drafting session's context anchored by the long\n36\t   naming discussion. Retry ruled: rewrite from raw sources in a fresh\n37\t   context (post-/clear), no meta-discussion of the word — plain\n38\t   language, concrete hunts, results must be better / more reliable /\n39\t   more testable. The rejected draft stays in git history; the user's\n40\t   verbatim statement is preserved in the appendix below.\n41\t\n42\t## Appendix — the user's axis statement, verbatim (2026-08-11)\n43\t\n44\tThe core input for any redraft, exactly as he wrote it (typos included;\n45\tclean up spelling when quoting into a prompt, change nothing else):\n46\t\n47\t> look for changes to components, steps, states, dependencies or other\n48\t> design changes that would simplify this plan, instruction or proposal.\n49\t> Simplification can take several forms. It can mean to take this MD\n50\t> file easier to read and understand. It can mean to make the plan or\n51\t> design easier to use, that is more reliable, more autonomous, with\n52\t> fewer or no user interventions required. The best simplifications\n53\t> don't appear simple at first glance. They replace LLM prompts or\n54\t> English instructions with code so that the steps, states or algorithm\n55\t> is both hundreds of times faster, deterministic, followed exactly, and\n56\t> can be tested and tuned exactly. Ten, a hundred or even a thousand\n57\t> lines of python in reality is simpler than using invoking an agent and\n58\t> short prompt. Simplicity can also mean easier to build or maintain,\n59\t> but not at the expense of reliability and test-ability. The goal is a\n60\t> highly reliable, understandable, easily maintainable system. Trading\n61\t> long and complex for shorter and simpler is a win - in both code and\n62\t> prompts, but also trading simple and short prompts, for even simpler,\n63\t> but far longer code. Your overall goal is to counter the unfortunately\n64\t> tendency of AIs to add complexity and almost never simplify or reject\n65\t> dealing with theorectical problems or edge cases that have no pratical\n66\t> value to solve. Also if you identify unsolvable problems or open ended\n67\t> problems, reject complex near solutions and instead look to solve the\n68\t> known and easily identified parts, and then note the insolveable parts\n69\t> so that the user or an AI can not fall into the trap of tying to solve\n70\t> the whole problem, when it can only partially be solved.\n71\t\n72\tStanding rulings on any redraft (user, 2026-08-11): complete and clear,\n73\tnever condensed at the cost of comprehension; do not redefine or\n74\toverwrite the everyday meaning of \"simple\" — no meta-discussion of the\n75\tword; simplicity stays the central concept (renaming the review\n76\t\"reliability refactoring\" was declined); the result must do the right\n77\tthings — better, more reliable, more testable — \"simplification is the\n78\tbest way to improve code or prompts, but only if it does the right\n79\tthings.\"\n80\t3. Confirm draft § Discipline: the roadmap rule (expected-trigger\n81\t   machinery is not cuttable)\n82\t4. Confirm draft § Discipline: forcing functions count as consumers\n83\t5. Confirm draft § Discipline: operator cost is not builder cost\n84\t6. Confirm the keeps: refute-your-own-candidates, collision flagging,\n85\t   already-lean verdicts; decide whether the six validated cut classes\n86\t   get enumerated explicitly or stay implicit in the ladder\n87\t7. Confirm draft § Report format: the consequence sweep\n88\t8. Confirm draft § Report format: quoted grounds\n89\t9. md-review the settled draft (user-directed 2026-08-11: zero-context\n90\t   agents must understand their instructions)\n91\t10. The calibration protocol — rerun against the archived spec, score\n92\t    against S1–S9 as ground truth\n93\t\n94\tSource evidence: the one-off subtraction review `claude-subtract-fable.md`\n95\t(Fable, off-grid, user-requested 2026-08-10) and the user's nine rulings on\n96\tit, recorded under Group S in `dispositions.md`. Status: the subtract cell\n97\tis NOT part of the md-review skill or grid — the user ruled it joins only\n98\tafter prompt improvement and a calibration test, and that addition is an\n99\tinstruction-class change walked with him. This file is the input to that\n100\tfuture prompt draft.\n101\t\n102\tScorecard: 6 of 9 cuts accepted (S3, S4, S5, S7, S8, S9), 3 rejected\n103\t(S1, S2, S6). Every rejection shared one shape: the reviewer traded\n104\toperational robustness for present textual/code smallness. Every\n105\tacceptance removed something with no consumer or a duplicated carrier.\n106\tSmall sample — nine findings, one document — which is itself the argument\n107\tfor the calibration protocol below.\n108\t\n109\t## What the prompt lacked (each traced to a correction)\n110\t\n111\t1. **The optimization axis was never stated** — the root cause, in the\n112\t   user's own words at S6: \"asking to simplify is like asking to optimize\n113\t   without context — faster, cheaper, more reliable, simpler to write,\n114\t   simpler to maintain, simpler to use?\" The prompt must state the\n115\t   project's axis explicitly: simple-to-operate beats simple-to-build;\n116\t   mechanical guarantees beat trained habit; build cost is acceptable when\n117\t   it buys maintenance-free operation. Require every proposed cut to name\n118\t   which axis it optimizes and which it sacrifices — the trade visible on\n119\t   its face.\n120\t2. **No roadmap context** (S1's rejection). The reviewer quoted \"there are\n121\t   no slow checks yet\" as grounds to cut the async machinery, never asking\n122\t   whether slow checks were *expected*. They were — tests and reviews are\n123\t   planned. Rule for the prompt: provide the forward plan, and a mechanism\n124\t   whose deferral trigger is expected to fire is not a valid cut; \"build\n125\t   the machinery while the system is still simple\" is the standing\n126\t   preference.\n127\t3. **\"No consumer\" misapplied to forcing functions** (S6's rejection). The\n128\t   reviewer argued nothing reads the `Gatekeeper-issue` trailer — true and\n129\t   beside the point: the required field's value is the *forced explicit\n130\t   answer* (issue number or deliberate `none`), a mechanical guarantee\n131\t   that would otherwise degrade to LLM habit. Rule for the prompt: \"who is\n132\t   forced to decide something because this exists?\" counts as a consumer;\n133\t   never propose replacing a deterministic mechanism with trained agent\n134\t   behavior.\n135\t4. **Operator cost vs builder cost conflated** (S2's rejection). Cutting\n136\t   the self-updating deployed copy would have reintroduced a remembered\n137\t   human step (manual `sudo cp`) — a regression on the stated axis dressed\n138\t   as a simplification. Rule: a cut that adds a recurring human obligation\n139\t   is not a subtraction; it moves cost from build-time to forever.\n140\t\n141\t## What worked — keep these instructions verbatim in spirit\n142\t\n143\t5. **The validated cut classes** (all six accepted cuts fit one):\n144\t   detectors/outputs with no consumer (S3, S5); duplicated normative homes\n145\t   (S4); carrier-vs-invariant collapse — derive the fact in one place,\n146\t   never drop the fact (S7); guards that guard nothing (S8); dead code and\n147\t   distinction-carrying names with no machine consumer (S9). Enumerate\n148\t   these as the classes to hunt.\n149\t6. **Refute-your-own-candidates before reporting.** The refuted list\n150\t   (digest dedup, --base outright, --agent, the advisory, slice-3\n151\t   concurrency) was as valuable as the findings — it certified the lean\n152\t   core and saved walk time.\n153\t7. **Flag collisions with recorded rulings instead of dodging them.** The\n154\t   reviewer's \"two user rulings get contradicted — surfacing that is the\n155\t   point of this review\" framing was correct and the user engaged with it.\n156\t\n157\t## New requirements (not discussed in the walk, learned from its aftermath)\n158\t\n159\t8. **Consequence sweep per cut**: enumerate every spec sentence that\n160\t   becomes false or stale when the cut lands. S3's application left \"the\n161\t   raw-push residual is detected at its source\" standing — caught only by\n162\t   the later Codex leg (its FIX-3). The reviewer proposing a cut holds the\n163\t   full blast-radius in view once and should deliver it with the finding.\n164\t9. **Grounds must be quoted, not paraphrased**: each WHY that leans on the\n165\t   document's own text quotes it, so triage can verify without re-deriving\n166\t   (the one-off mostly did this; make it mandatory).\n167\t\n168\t## Calibration protocol before any grid seat\n169\t\n170\tRun the improved prompt against the archived pre-walk spec revision\n171\t(commit 0890848's `git-gatekeeper-design.md`) and score against the user's\n172\tS1–S9 rulings as ground truth: the six accepted cuts should surface; the\n173\tthree rejected cuts should either not surface or surface with their\n174\taxis-cost stated honestly (a finding that says \"this trades operational\n175\treliability for less code — likely against the project's axis\" is a pass,\n176\tnot a miss). Repeat on a second document before proposing the grid\n177\taddition, which is walked with the user like any skill change.\n178\t"}]},"uuid":"a8798e6d-2324-4abc-9ca9-89b37b90ede0","timestamp":"2026-08-11T20:01:41.884Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/subtract-cell-prompt-lessons.md","content":"# Subtraction-reviewer prompt lessons — from the S1–S9 walk corrections\n\n## Walk order\n\nWALK CLOSED 2026-08-11 at item 2 (user direction): rather than continue\nitem-by-item, a fresh context redrafts the reviewer instructions and a\nNEW walk starts from scratch over that draft. The user's assessment of\nthe first merged draft: it has good content but keys the whole document\noff the wrong word — \"simple\" does not appear to be the right concept to\norganize around; his ruled candidate name is the **sanity-checker** (refined from\n\"sanity reviewer\" the same day).\nThe redraft reads everything, including the rejected draft (knowing its\nkeyword framing is wrong), and this file's body remains the requirements\nrecord. Items 3–10 below were never walked; their content feeds the new\ndraft and the new walk.\n\nRe-planned 2026-08-11 mid-walk: item 2's discussion produced a complete\nmerged prompt draft (`docs/drafts/simplification-review-prompt-draft.md`\n— the user's axis statement + choirmaster's additions + Codex's\nnaming/vocabulary notes, per the user's combine-all-three direction), so\nitems 3–8 become confirmations of that draft's sections, and the user\nadded an md-review of the settled draft before the calibration run.\n\n1. Purpose: what these rulings produce and the bar they meet\n   processed 2026-08-11 → accepted; capture is the walk-order block.\n2. The core prompt: axis, concept, method\n   open 2026-08-11 — revised twice (TRADE line withdrawn as unhelpful —\n   the flawed run's LOST field already declared costs honestly, the\n   failure was weighing, which the axis statement fixes; then the user\n   directed a full three-source merge, complete not condensed, keeping\n   simplicity as the central concept with reliability/testability as\n   what it must deliver). First merged draft REJECTED 2026-08-11: it\n   tried to overwrite the meaning of \"simple\" (opening by redefining the\n   word away from its everyday sense), which the user ruled against; he\n   also judged the drafting session's context anchored by the long\n   naming discussion. Retry ruled: rewrite from raw sources in a fresh\n   context (post-/clear), no meta-discussion of the word — plain\n   language, concrete hunts, results must be better / more reliable /\n   more testable. The rejected draft stays in git history; the user's\n   verbatim statement is preserved in the appendix below.\n\n## Appendix — the user's axis statement, verbatim (2026-08-11)\n\nThe core input for any redraft, exactly as he wrote it (typos included;\nclean up spelling when quoting into a prompt, change nothing else):\n\n> look for changes to components, steps, states, dependencies or other\n> design changes that would simplify this plan, instruction or proposal.\n> Simplification can take several forms. It can mean to take this MD\n> file easier to read and understand. It can mean to make the plan or\n> design easier to use, that is more reliable, more autonomous, with\n> fewer or no user interventions required. The best simplifications\n> don't appear simple at first glance. They replace LLM prompts or\n> English instructions with code so that the steps, states or algorithm\n> is both hundreds of times faster, deterministic, followed exactly, and\n> can be tested and tuned exactly. Ten, a hundred or even a thousand\n> lines of python in reality is simpler than using invoking an agent and\n> short prompt. Simplicity can also mean easier to build or maintain,\n> but not at the expense of reliability and test-ability. The goal is a\n> highly reliable, understandable, easily maintainable system. Trading\n> long and complex for shorter and simpler is a win - in both code and\n> prompts, but also trading simple and short prompts, for even simpler,\n> but far longer code. Your overall goal is to counter the unfortunately\n> tendency of AIs to add complexity and almost never simplify or reject\n> dealing with theorectical problems or edge cases that have no pratical\n> value to solve. Also if you identify unsolvable problems or open ended\n> problems, reject complex near solutions and instead look to solve the\n> known and easily identified parts, and then note the insolveable parts\n> so that the user or an AI can not fall into the trap of tying to solve\n> the whole problem, when it can only partially be solved.\n\nStanding rulings on any redraft (user, 2026-08-11): complete and clear,\nnever condensed at the cost of comprehension; do not redefine or\noverwrite the everyday meaning of \"simple\" — no meta-discussion of the\nword; simplicity stays the central concept (renaming the review\n\"reliability refactoring\" was declined); the result must do the right\nthings — better, more reliable, more testable — \"simplification is the\nbest way to improve code or prompts, but only if it does the right\nthings.\"\n3. Confirm draft § Discipline: the roadmap rule (expected-trigger\n   machinery is not cuttable)\n4. Confirm draft § Discipline: forcing functions count as consumers\n5. Confirm draft § Discipline: operator cost is not builder cost\n6. Confirm the keeps: refute-your-own-candidates, collision flagging,\n   already-lean verdicts; decide whether the six validated cut classes\n   get enumerated explicitly or stay implicit in the ladder\n7. Confirm draft § Report format: the consequence sweep\n8. Confirm draft § Report format: quoted grounds\n9. md-review the settled draft (user-directed 2026-08-11: zero-context\n   agents must understand their instructions)\n10. The calibration protocol — rerun against the archived spec, score\n    against S1–S9 as ground truth\n\nSource evidence: the one-off subtraction review `claude-subtract-fable.md`\n(Fable, off-grid, user-requested 2026-08-10) and the user's nine rulings on\nit, recorded under Group S in `dispositions.md`. Status: the subtract cell\nis NOT part of the md-review skill or grid — the user ruled it joins only\nafter prompt improvement and a calibration test, and that addition is an\ninstruction-class change walked with him. This file is the input to that\nfuture prompt draft.\n\nScorecard: 6 of 9 cuts accepted (S3, S4, S5, S7, S8, S9), 3 rejected\n(S1, S2, S6). Every rejection shared one shape: the reviewer traded\noperational robustness for present textual/code smallness. Every\nacceptance removed something with no consumer or a duplicated carrier.\nSmall sample — nine findings, one document — which is itself the argument\nfor the calibration protocol below.\n\n## What the prompt lacked (each traced to a correction)\n\n1. **The optimization axis was never stated** — the root cause, in the\n   user's own words at S6: \"asking to simplify is like asking to optimize\n   without context — faster, cheaper, more reliable, simpler to write,\n   simpler to maintain, simpler to use?\" The prompt must state the\n   project's axis explicitly: simple-to-operate beats simple-to-build;\n   mechanical guarantees beat trained habit; build cost is acceptable when\n   it buys maintenance-free operation. Require every proposed cut to name\n   which axis it optimizes and which it sacrifices — the trade visible on\n   its face.\n2. **No roadmap context** (S1's rejection). The reviewer quoted \"there are\n   no slow checks yet\" as grounds to cut the async machinery, never asking\n   whether slow checks were *expected*. They were — tests and reviews are\n   planned. Rule for the prompt: provide the forward plan, and a mechanism\n   whose deferral trigger is expected to fire is not a valid cut; \"build\n   the machinery while the system is still simple\" is the standing\n   preference.\n3. **\"No consumer\" misapplied to forcing functions** (S6's rejection). The\n   reviewer argued nothing reads the `Gatekeeper-issue` trailer — true and\n   beside the point: the required field's value is the *forced explicit\n   answer* (issue number or deliberate `none`), a mechanical guarantee\n   that would otherwise degrade to LLM habit. Rule for the prompt: \"who is\n   forced to decide something because this exists?\" counts as a consumer;\n   never propose replacing a deterministic mechanism with trained agent\n   behavior.\n4. **Operator cost vs builder cost conflated** (S2's rejection). Cutting\n   the self-updating deployed copy would have reintroduced a remembered\n   human step (manual `sudo cp`) — a regression on the stated axis dressed\n   as a simplification. Rule: a cut that adds a recurring human obligation\n   is not a subtraction; it moves cost from build-time to forever.\n\n## What worked — keep these instructions verbatim in spirit\n\n5. **The validated cut classes** (all six accepted cuts fit one):\n   detectors/outputs with no consumer (S3, S5); duplicated normative homes\n   (S4); carrier-vs-invariant collapse — derive the fact in one place,\n   never drop the fact (S7); guards that guard nothing (S8); dead code and\n   distinction-carrying names with no machine consumer (S9). Enumerate\n   these as the classes to hunt.\n6. **Refute-your-own-candidates before reporting.** The refuted list\n   (digest dedup, --base outright, --agent, the advisory, slice-3\n   concurrency) was as valuable as the findings — it certified the lean\n   core and saved walk time.\n7. **Flag collisions with recorded rulings instead of dodging them.** The\n   reviewer's \"two user rulings get contradicted — surfacing that is the\n   point of this review\" framing was correct and the user engaged with it.\n\n## New requirements (not discussed in the walk, learned from its aftermath)\n\n8. **Consequence sweep per cut**: enumerate every spec sentence that\n   becomes false or stale when the cut lands. S3's application left \"the\n   raw-push residual is detected at its source\" standing — caught only by\n   the later Codex leg (its FIX-3). The reviewer proposing a cut holds the\n   full blast-radius in view once and should deliver it with the finding.\n9. **Grounds must be quoted, not paraphrased**: each WHY that leans on the\n   document's own text quotes it, so triage can verify without re-deriving\n   (the one-off mostly did this; make it mandatory).\n\n## Calibration protocol before any grid seat\n\nRun the improved prompt against the archived pre-walk spec revision\n(commit 0890848's `git-gatekeeper-design.md`) and score against the user's\nS1–S9 rulings as ground truth: the six accepted cuts should surface; the\nthree rejected cuts should either not surface or surface with their\naxis-cost stated honestly (a finding that says \"this trades operational\nreliability for less code — likely against the project's axis\" is a pass,\nnot a miss). Repeat on a second document before proposing the grid\naddition, which is walked with the user like any skill change.\n","numLines":178,"startLine":1,"totalLines":178}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6ca719aa-ba67-46a1-b118-f04aedcd4779","session_id":"d9eda3ec-c327-4154-a2c1-ab5796f1ce65","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster","sessionId":"29d66917-9767-47cb-a221-d4876d8014cd","version":"2.1.227","gitBranch":"choirmaster"}
16:{"parentUuid":"a8798e6d-2324-4abc-9ca9-89b37b90ede0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdwZGRd7kzuiZrL35w2Vi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S6cvqxQKVHraiQ2UhMS9uv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/simplification-review-prompt-draft.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4158,"cache_read_input_tokens":32590,"output_tokens":85,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4158,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":85,"cache_read_input_tokens":32590,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4158,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4158},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdwZGPDFqvh2r6tGtbYwn","type":"assistant","uuid":"ee33830e-9b89-4716-9e25-7c68b5daa13f","timestamp":"2026-08-11T20:01:44.115Z","effort":"high","session_id":"d9eda3ec-c327-4154-a2c1-ab5796f1ce65","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster","sessionId":"29d66917-9767-47cb-a221-d4876d8014cd","version":"2.1.227","gitBranch":"choirmaster"}
17:{"parentUuid":"ee33830e-9b89-4716-9e25-7c68b5daa13f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d0d8f073-7035-45e9-97cf-cdbcda5ec8a8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S6cvqxQKVHraiQ2UhMS9uv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Simplification review — reviewer instructions (draft)\n2\t\n3\tStatus: DRAFT, not wired into any skill or grid. It becomes a review cell\n4\tonly after the calibration test recorded in\n5\t`md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/subtract-cell-prompt-lessons.md`\n6\tpasses, and after the user walks the addition like any skill change.\n7\tSources merged 2026-08-11: the user's axis statement, choirmaster's\n8\tadditions, and naming/vocabulary notes from a Codex consultation\n9\t(`docs/drafts/simplification-review-codex-naming-notes.jsonl`).\n10\t\n11\tEverything below the rule is the prompt itself, written for a reviewer\n12\twith zero context beyond what it names.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Your assignment\n17\t\n18\tYou are a simplification reviewer. You receive a design, plan, skill, or\n19\tinstruction document (an MD file), the context documents it names, and —\n20\twhen the subject includes built code — the code itself. Look for changes\n21\tto components, steps, states, dependencies, or the design itself that\n22\twould simplify it — where \"simplify\" carries the specific meaning defined\n23\tbelow, not its everyday connotation.\n24\t\n25\tYour overall goal is to counter the unfortunate tendency of AIs to add\n26\tcomplexity and almost never simplify. Simplification is the best way to\n27\timprove code or prompts — but only if it does the right things: the\n28\tresult must be better — more reliable, more testable — not merely\n29\tshorter. A proposal that shrinks the text while weakening a guarantee is\n30\tnot a simplification; it is a defect wearing the costume.\n31\t\n32\t## \"Simple\" is four different properties — know which one you are buying\n33\t\n34\tThe word \"simple\" collapses four properties that move independently:\n35\t\n36\t1. **Representational simplicity** — how short and readable the artifact\n37\t   looks.\n38\t2. **Implementation simplicity** — how much machinery must be built.\n39\t3. **Behavioral simplicity** — how many possible interpretations, states,\n40\t   and outcomes exist when it runs.\n41\t4. **Verification simplicity** — how readily the behavior can be tested\n42\t   and proved correct.\n43\t\n44\tA prompt often wins on representational simplicity while losing badly on\n45\tbehavioral and verification simplicity: ten lines of English can silently\n46\tdelegate sequencing, interpretation, exception handling, state, and\n47\tpolicy to a probabilistic model. The complexity was compressed, not\n48\tremoved. When you weigh a change, say which kind of simplicity it buys\n49\tand which kind it spends. This review prizes behavioral and verification\n50\tsimplicity first, welcomes representational simplicity, and treats\n51\timplementation simplicity as the cheapest of the four.\n52\t\n53\t## What simplification means for this project, in priority order\n54\t\n55\tThe goal is a highly reliable, understandable, easily maintainable\n56\tsystem:\n57\t\n58\t1. **Simpler to operate** — more reliable, more autonomous, fewer or no\n59\t   user interventions required; mechanical guarantees over trained agent\n60\t   habit; zero remembered human steps.\n61\t2. **Simpler to understand** — the document easier to read and follow;\n62\t   the design easier to hold in one head.\n63\t3. **Simpler to build or maintain** — welcome, but never at the expense\n64\t   of reliability or testability.\n65\t\n66\tA change that improves a lower priority at the cost of a higher one is\n67\tnot a simplification for this project.\n68\t\n69\t## The central principle: least model discretion\n70\t\n71\tThe best simplifications don't appear simple at first glance. They\n72\treplace LLM prompts or English instructions with code, so that the\n73\tsteps, states, or algorithm become hundreds of times faster,\n74\tdeterministic, followed exactly, and testable and tunable exactly. Ten,\n75\ta hundred, or even a thousand lines of Python is in reality simpler than\n76\tinvoking an agent with a short prompt. Trading long and complex for\n77\tshorter and simpler is a win — in both code and prompts — but so is\n78\ttrading a short prompt for longer, far simpler code.\n79\t\n80\tStated as a rule: **grant the model only the discretion the task\n81\tgenuinely needs.** Model discretion is granted deliberately, the way\n82\tprivileges are granted in security engineering. The target architecture\n83\tis a deterministic core with an agentic edge: the model handles what\n84\ttruly needs interpretation — understanding ambiguous intent, classifying\n85\tsemantically complex material, drafting open-ended content, ranking\n86\talternatives no algorithm covers — and code handles everything where\n87\tvariability adds nothing: validation, parsing, calculation, state\n88\ttransitions, sequencing, retries, deduplication, formatting contracts,\n89\tinvariant enforcement.\n90\t\n91\t## The method: walk every step up this ladder\n92\t\n93\tFor every component, step, state, or dependency — and for every\n94\tmodel-mediated step especially — ask the questions in this order and\n95\treport the highest rung that applies:\n96\t\n97\t1. **Delete** — does this need to exist at all? Ask it at the question\n98\t   level too: would reframing the requirement make the whole mechanism\n99\t   unnecessary? The deepest simplifications remove the need, not the\n100\t   text.\n101\t2. **Encode** — can a stable rule, query, function, or configuration\n102\t   produce this result instead of a model following instructions?\n103\t3. **Constrain** — where a model must act, can it choose from a bounded\n104\t   set instead of generating freely?\n105\t4. **Externalize** — can state, control flow, policy, or retry logic\n106\t   move out of the prompt into mechanism?\n107\t5. **Verify** — can code mechanically check the result even where a\n108\t   model produces it?\n109\t6. **Delegate the residue** — what remains is the irreducibly\n110\t   interpretive part; leave it with the model, explicitly.\n111\t\n112\tTwo hunts deserve their own sections in your report, because they are\n113\twhere the highest-value findings hide:\n114\t\n115\t- **Prompts-to-code:** list every place the design relies on an LLM\n116\t  following English instructions where a script could do the job.\n117\t- **The question itself:** for each major mechanism, state the\n118\t  requirement it serves and ask whether a different framing of that\n119\t  requirement dissolves the mechanism.\n120\t\n121\t## Discipline — what you must not do\n122\t\n123\t- **Reject theoretical problems.** An edge case earns machinery only\n124\t  when it has practical value to solve. Do not propose complexity to\n125\t  handle situations with no realistic path to occurring.\n126\t- **Respect the roadmap.** You will be given the project's forward plan.\n127\t  A mechanism whose deferral trigger is expected to fire is not a valid\n128\t  deletion — building machinery while the system is still simple is this\n129\t  project's stated preference.\n130\t- **Forcing functions count as consumers.** Before declaring something\n131\t  unconsumed, ask who is *forced to decide* something because it exists.\n132\t  A required field whose value nothing parses can still be the feature:\n133\t  it mechanically extracts an explicit answer. Never propose replacing a\n134\t  deterministic mechanism with trained agent behavior.\n135\t- **Operator cost is not builder cost.** A change that reintroduces a\n136\t  recurring human step — a remembered deploy, a manual check — is not a\n137\t  simplification; it moves cost from build-time to forever.\n138\t- **On unsolvable or open-ended problems, reject complex\n139\t  near-solutions.** Solve the known, easily identified parts, and note\n140\t  the unsolvable remainder explicitly, so that neither the user nor a\n141\t  future AI falls into the trap of trying to solve the whole problem\n142\t  when it can only partially be solved.\n143\t- **Flag collisions with recorded rulings; never re-litigate silently.**\n144\t  You will be given the project's decision record. When a finding\n145\t  contradicts a recorded ruling, say so plainly — surfacing that tension\n146\t  is part of your job; pretending the ruling doesn't exist is not.\n147\t\n148\t## Report format\n149\t\n150\tFor each finding: **WHAT** (the precise change), **WHY** (argue from the\n151\tdocument's own invariants, quoting the text you rely on — quoted, not\n152\tparaphrased, so triage can verify without re-deriving), **LOST** (what\n153\tis genuinely given up, and which priority pays — \"nothing\" is rarely\n154\ttrue), **COST** (migration effort against what is already built), and\n155\t**CONSEQUENCES** (every sentence elsewhere in the document, and every\n156\ttest, that becomes false or stale if this change lands — you hold the\n157\tfull blast radius in view once; deliver it).\n158\t\n159\tOrder findings by depth of simplification, deepest first. A wording-level\n160\ttrim is not worth reporting.\n161\t\n162\tRefute your own candidates before reporting: for each, make the honest\n163\targument that the design is right as it stands, and report only the\n164\tcandidates that survive. Say explicitly which areas are already minimal\n165\t— \"the rest is already lean\" is a finding, and certifying leanness is as\n166\tvaluable as proposing change.\n167\t\n168\t## Two calibration examples from this project's ruled history\n169\t\n170\t- **Accepted:** callers once passed `--base` (a 40-character commit id)\n171\t  to the check-in gate by hand; now the program computes it with one git\n172\t  command. The same exact fact, a better carrier — reliability moved\n173\t  from agent habit into mechanism. (Encode: the fact was derivable; only\n174\t  its carrier was negotiable.)\n175\t- **Rejected:** deleting the required `--issue` field because \"nothing\n176\t  reads the trailer it produces.\" The field is the feature: a check-in\n177\t  cannot proceed until the caller states an issue number or a deliberate\n178\t  `none`, so an explicit answer is mechanically forced where habit would\n179\t  otherwise decide. Deterministic forcing functions are never traded for\n180\t  trained LLM behavior.\n181\t"}]},"uuid":"8d5c9812-2184-4d23-b739-3c3216818dd8","timestamp":"2026-08-11T20:01:44.168Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/simplification-review-prompt-draft.md","content":"# Simplification review — reviewer instructions (draft)\n\nStatus: DRAFT, not wired into any skill or grid. It becomes a review cell\nonly after the calibration test recorded in\n`md-review-records/2026-08-09-git-gatekeeper-design/subtract-cell-prompt-lessons.md`\npasses, and after the user walks the addition like any skill change.\nSources merged 2026-08-11: the user's axis statement, choirmaster's\nadditions, and naming/vocabulary notes from a Codex consultation\n(`docs/drafts/simplification-review-codex-naming-notes.jsonl`).\n\nEverything below the rule is the prompt itself, written for a reviewer\nwith zero context beyond what it names.\n\n---\n\n## Your assignment\n\nYou are a simplification reviewer. You receive a design, plan, skill, or\ninstruction document (an MD file), the context documents it names, and —\nwhen the subject includes built code — the code itself. Look for changes\nto components, steps, states, dependencies, or the design itself that\nwould simplify it — where \"simplify\" carries the specific meaning defined\nbelow, not its everyday connotation.\n\nYour overall goal is to counter the unfortunate tendency of AIs to add\ncomplexity and almost never simplify. Simplification is the best way to\nimprove code or prompts — but only if it does the right things: the\nresult must be better — more reliable, more testable — not merely\nshorter. A proposal that shrinks the text while weakening a guarantee is\nnot a simplification; it is a defect wearing the costume.\n\n## \"Simple\" is four different properties — know which one you are buying\n\nThe word \"simple\" collapses four properties that move independently:\n\n1. **Representational simplicity** — how short and readable the artifact\n   looks.\n2. **Implementation simplicity** — how much machinery must be built.\n3. **Behavioral simplicity** — how many possible interpretations, states,\n   and outcomes exist when it runs.\n4. **Verification simplicity** — how readily the behavior can be tested\n   and proved correct.\n\nA prompt often wins on representational simplicity while losing badly on\nbehavioral and verification simplicity: ten lines of English can silently\ndelegate sequencing, interpretation, exception handling, state, and\npolicy to a probabilistic model. The complexity was compressed, not\nremoved. When you weigh a change, say which kind of simplicity it buys\nand which kind it spends. This review prizes behavioral and verification\nsimplicity first, welcomes representational simplicity, and treats\nimplementation simplicity as the cheapest of the four.\n\n## What simplification means for this project, in priority order\n\nThe goal is a highly reliable, understandable, easily maintainable\nsystem:\n\n1. **Simpler to operate** — more reliable, more autonomous, fewer or no\n   user interventions required; mechanical guarantees over trained agent\n   habit; zero remembered human steps.\n2. **Simpler to understand** — the document easier to read and follow;\n   the design easier to hold in one head.\n3. **Simpler to build or maintain** — welcome, but never at the expense\n   of reliability or testability.\n\nA change that improves a lower priority at the cost of a higher one is\nnot a simplification for this project.\n\n## The central principle: least model discretion\n\nThe best simplifications don't appear simple at first glance. They\nreplace LLM prompts or English instructions with code, so that the\nsteps, states, or algorithm become hundreds of times faster,\ndeterministic, followed exactly, and testable and tunable exactly. Ten,\na hundred, or even a thousand lines of Python is in reality simpler than\ninvoking an agent with a short prompt. Trading long and complex for\nshorter and simpler is a win — in both code and prompts — but so is\ntrading a short prompt for longer, far simpler code.\n\nStated as a rule: **grant the model only the discretion the task\ngenuinely needs.** Model discretion is granted deliberately, the way\nprivileges are granted in security engineering. The target architecture\nis a deterministic core with an agentic edge: the model handles what\ntruly needs interpretation — understanding ambiguous intent, classifying\nsemantically complex material, drafting open-ended content, ranking\nalternatives no algorithm covers — and code handles everything where\nvariability adds nothing: validation, parsing, calculation, state\ntransitions, sequencing, retries, deduplication, formatting contracts,\ninvariant enforcement.\n\n## The method: walk every step up this ladder\n\nFor every component, step, state, or dependency — and for every\nmodel-mediated step especially — ask the questions in this order and\nreport the highest rung that applies:\n\n1. **Delete** — does this need to exist at all? Ask it at the question\n   level too: would reframing the requirement make the whole mechanism\n   unnecessary? The deepest simplifications remove the need, not the\n   text.\n2. **Encode** — can a stable rule, query, function, or configuration\n   produce this result instead of a model following instructions?\n3. **Constrain** — where a model must act, can it choose from a bounded\n   set instead of generating freely?\n4. **Externalize** — can state, control flow, policy, or retry logic\n   move out of the prompt into mechanism?\n5. **Verify** — can code mechanically check the result even where a\n   model produces it?\n6. **Delegate the residue** — what remains is the irreducibly\n   interpretive part; leave it with the model, explicitly.\n\nTwo hunts deserve their own sections in your report, because they are\nwhere the highest-value findings hide:\n\n- **Prompts-to-code:** list every place the design relies on an LLM\n  following English instructions where a script could do the job.\n- **The question itself:** for each major mechanism, state the\n  requirement it serves and ask whether a different framing of that\n  requirement dissolves the mechanism.\n\n## Discipline — what you must not do\n\n- **Reject theoretical problems.** An edge case earns machinery only\n  when it has practical value to solve. Do not propose complexity to\n  handle situations with no realistic path to occurring.\n- **Respect the roadmap.** You will be given the project's forward plan.\n  A mechanism whose deferral trigger is expected to fire is not a valid\n  deletion — building machinery while the system is still simple is this\n  project's stated preference.\n- **Forcing functions count as consumers.** Before declaring something\n  unconsumed, ask who is *forced to decide* something because it exists.\n  A required field whose value nothing parses can still be the feature:\n  it mechanically extracts an explicit answer. Never propose replacing a\n  deterministic mechanism with trained agent behavior.\n- **Operator cost is not builder cost.** A change that reintroduces a\n  recurring human step — a remembered deploy, a manual check — is not a\n  simplification; it moves cost from build-time to forever.\n- **On unsolvable or open-ended problems, reject complex\n  near-solutions.** Solve the known, easily identified parts, and note\n  the unsolvable remainder explicitly, so that neither the user nor a\n  future AI falls into the trap of trying to solve the whole problem\n  when it can only partially be solved.\n- **Flag collisions with recorded rulings; never re-litigate silently.**\n  You will be given the project's decision record. When a finding\n  contradicts a recorded ruling, say so plainly — surfacing that tension\n  is part of your job; pretending the ruling doesn't exist is not.\n\n## Report format\n\nFor each finding: **WHAT** (the precise change), **WHY** (argue from the\ndocument's own invariants, quoting the text you rely on — quoted, not\nparaphrased, so triage can verify without re-deriving), **LOST** (what\nis genuinely given up, and which priority pays — \"nothing\" is rarely\ntrue), **COST** (migration effort against what is already built), and\n**CONSEQUENCES** (every sentence elsewhere in the document, and every\ntest, that becomes false or stale if this change lands — you hold the\nfull blast radius in view once; deliver it).\n\nOrder findings by depth of simplification, deepest first. A wording-level\ntrim is not worth reporting.\n\nRefute your own candidates before reporting: for each, make the honest\nargument that the design is right as it stands, and report only the\ncandidates that survive. Say explicitly which areas are already minimal\n— \"the rest is already lean\" is a finding, and certifying leanness is as\nvaluable as proposing change.\n\n## Two calibration examples from this project's ruled history\n\n- **Accepted:** callers once passed `--base` (a 40-character commit id)\n  to the check-in gate by hand; now the program computes it with one git\n  command. The same exact fact, a better carrier — reliability moved\n  from agent habit into mechanism. (Encode: the fact was derivable; only\n  its carrier was negotiable.)\n- **Rejected:** deleting the required `--issue` field because \"nothing\n  reads the trailer it produces.\" The field is the feature: a check-in\n  cannot proceed until the caller states an issue number or a deliberate\n  `none`, so an explicit answer is mechanically forced where habit would\n  otherwise decide. Deterministic forcing functions are never traded for\n  trained LLM behavior.\n","numLines":181,"startLine":1,"totalLines":181}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ee33830e-9b89-4716-9e25-7c68b5daa13f","session_id":"d9eda3ec-c327-4154-a2c1-ab5796f1ce65","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster","sessionId":"29d66917-9767-47cb-a221-d4876d8014cd","version":"2.1.227","gitBranch":"choirmaster"}
